From: Johan Alvarado <contact@c127.dev>
Date: Thu, 11 Sep 2026 18:44:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] spi: spi-qpic-snand: publish the ECC context to snandc->qspi

qcom_spi_ooblayout_ecc() and qcom_spi_ooblayout_free() read the ECC
configuration through snandc->qspi->ecc, which qcom_spi_probe() points at a
zeroed scratch struct. qcom_spi_ecc_init_ctx_pipelined() installs the
ooblayout but never publishes the context it just allocated, and
qcom_spi_ecc_cleanup_ctx_pipelined() frees that context.

So the ooblayout callbacks never see the real configuration. On a first probe
they read the zeroed scratch; on a re-probe after a failure they read through a
pointer to the context that cleanup already freed. Those are two different
states and only the second is genuinely indeterminate, which is why the
resulting failure is intermittent rather than constant.

On an IPQ5018 board with an ESMT F50D1G41LB SPI-NAND, roughly half of all boots
ended with the NAND never appearing:

  spi-nand spi0.0: probe with driver spi-nand failed with error -512
  UBI error: cannot open mtd rootfs, error -2
  Waiting for root device /dev/ubiblock0_1...

Publish the context when it is created, clear the pointer when it is freed, and
drop the scratch allocation and the late assignment in the prepare_io_req path
that only existed to paper over the missing publication.

Fixes: 7304d1909080 ("spi: spi-qpic: add driver for QCOM SPI NAND flash Interface")

[Local backport note, not part of the upstream submission: this carries the two
 upstream patches squashed into one, since they touch the same lines and the
 second has no functional effect on its own:
   f94c9b68bb5f ("spi: spi-qpic-snand: publish the ECC context to snandc->qspi")
   8d6911645478 ("spi: spi-qpic-snand: drop the redundant ECC context handling")
 Both were applied by Mark Brown to broonie/spi.git for-7.4 on 2026-09-18, so
 nothing is to be submitted for this - drop the patch once the kernel here
 carries them. The hashes are as quoted in the maintainer's reply on the v2
 thread; they have not been checked against a local clone of that tree.]
---
--- a/drivers/spi/spi-qpic-snand.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-qpic-snand.c
@@ -409,6 +409,8 @@
 	dev_dbg(snandc->dev, "ECC strength: %u bits per %u bytes\n",
 		ecc_cfg->strength, ecc_cfg->step_size);
 
+	snandc->qspi->ecc = ecc_cfg;
+
 	return 0;
 
 err_free_ecc_cfg:
@@ -418,8 +420,10 @@
 
 static void qcom_spi_ecc_cleanup_ctx_pipelined(struct nand_device *nand)
 {
+	struct qcom_nand_controller *snandc = nand_to_qcom_snand(nand);
 	struct qpic_ecc *ecc_cfg = nand_to_ecc_ctx(nand);
 
+	snandc->qspi->ecc = NULL;
 	kfree(ecc_cfg);
 }
 
@@ -427,9 +431,7 @@
 						 struct nand_page_io_req *req)
 {
 	struct qcom_nand_controller *snandc = nand_to_qcom_snand(nand);
-	struct qpic_ecc *ecc_cfg = nand_to_ecc_ctx(nand);
 
-	snandc->qspi->ecc = ecc_cfg;
 	snandc->qspi->raw_rw = false;
 	snandc->qspi->oob_rw = false;
 	snandc->qspi->page_rw = false;
@@ -1539,15 +1541,10 @@
 	struct spi_controller *ctlr;
 	struct qcom_nand_controller *snandc;
 	struct qpic_spi_nand *qspi;
-	struct qpic_ecc *ecc;
 	struct resource *res;
 	const void *dev_data;
 	int ret;
 
-	ecc = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*ecc), GFP_KERNEL);
-	if (!ecc)
-		return -ENOMEM;
-
 	qspi = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*qspi), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!qspi)
 		return -ENOMEM;
@@ -1564,7 +1561,6 @@
 	snandc->dev = dev;
 	snandc->qspi = qspi;
 	snandc->qspi->ctlr = ctlr;
-	snandc->qspi->ecc = ecc;
 
 	dev_data = of_device_get_match_data(dev);
 	if (!dev_data) {
